{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794588724,"nonce":"6eIKOFnkKIx9GdB2j5flnPZywbs=","title":"A render phase of 120 to 440 ms, once, in the frame that starts a FLIP slide","message":"Seen in two of about fourteen windowed runs of flip_window_check (release, macOS, rows mode): the frame that starts the first slide, a full paint with no rebuild, had a render phase (p4 in BLINC_MOTION_TRACE) of 436 ms in the first run and 123 ms in a run of a renamed copy of the binary. Its own breakdown does not hold the time: paint walker 126 us, collect 32 us, and the rest of render_tree_with_motion_opt (the log calls it gpu_us) 3.1 ms. So about 120 ms of that phase was spent outside the render call: in the style and animation pass at the start of phase 4, or in frame.present().\n\nNot reproduced since: six back-to-back runs, two runs straight after a rebuild, and four runs of fresh-named copies gave slide-start frames of 3.7 to 6.5 ms and no stall over 20 ms. The renamed copy run again at once gave 5.4 ms. So it is a one-off, tied to something about the first run (a cold cache of the system, the binary or the GPU compiler is the guess; nothing shows it).\n\nThe trace now records p4_done_us {styles, render}: offsets from the start of phase 4, so the next stall says whether it was styling, painting or presenting. To catch one: run the example with BLINC_MOTION_TRACE=\u003cfile\u003e and look for a drawn frame with p4 over 50000, then read its p4_done_us.\n\nRelated: 77fc97d (the window check where it was found).","files":null}]}